## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of basic demand paging, synchronization, and a tiny per-page state machine.

## Key Concepts
- Demand paging
- Per-page locking
- State machine: EMPTY, LOADING, READY
- Wait/wake and memory visibility

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: per-page fault path in a tiny OS
typedef enum { EMPTY, LOADING, READY } page_state_t;
typedef struct { page_state_t s; spinlock l; char *data; bool dirty; } page_t;
void handle_fault(page_t *p){
  acquire(&p->l);
  if (p->s==READY){ release(&p->l); return; }
  if (p->s==EMPTY){ p->s=LOADING; release(&p->l); read_disk_async(p); p->s=READY; wake_all(&p->wq); return; }
  while (p->s!=READY) wait(&p->wq, &p->l);
  release(&p->l);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test concurrent touches of the same page to ensure only a single IO is issued?
- How would you extend to multiple pages or prefetch while preserving correctness?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of fine-grained concurrency in a critical DB cache path and how to avoid redundant disk IO under contention.

## Key Concepts

- Per-block synchronization
- Atomic state machines (EMPTY/LOADING/LOADED)
- Condition variables / wait queues
- Memory visibility and ordering around I/O
- Correctness under concurrent access

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-C code: per-block cache entry
const BLOCK_SIZE = 4096;
const NUM_BLOCKS = 1024;
const STATE_EMPTY = 0, STATE_LOADING = 1, STATE_LOADED = 2;

struct Block {
  atomicInt state;
  char data[BLOCK_SIZE];
  bool dirty;
  Mutex m;        // for waiters
  CondVar cv;
};

Block cache[NUM_BLOCKS];

// fetchBlock(bid) pseudo
Block* fetchBlock(int bid) {
  Block* b = &cache[bid];
  if (atomicLoad(&b->state) == STATE_LOADED) return b;
  int expected = STATE_EMPTY;
  if (atomicCompareExchange(&b->state, &expected, STATE_LOADING)) {
    // load from disk
    readFromDisk(bid, b->data);
    atomicStore(&b->state, STATE_LOADED);
    signalAll(&b->cv);
  } else {
    // wait until loaded
    lock(&b->m);
    while (atomicLoad(&b->state) != STATE_LOADED) wait(&b->cv, &b->m);
    unlock(&b->m);
  }
  return b;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend for per-block eviction under memory pressure?
- How would you test correctness under high contention and slow disks?
